\section{Writing your own summary function}{

The entry point is \code{\link[=sizeIntegral]{sizeIntegral()}}. It selects the size range, applies the
bin-averaging appropriate to the model's \code{\link[=second_order_w]{second_order_w()}} setting and wraps
the result in the right mizer array class, so a summary function built on it
is automatically consistent with the quadrature the model is actually using.
Pass the whole weighting factor \eqn{K(w)} evaluated on the size grid, but
neither the bin widths \code{params@dw} nor any bin-averaging of your own:
\code{sizeIntegral()} supplies both.

If your quantity involves the predation kernel, take the kernel from
\code{\link[=encounter_kernel]{encounter_kernel()}} rather than from \code{\link[=pred_kernel]{pred_kernel()}}, and pair it with the
plain point prey weight \code{params@w_full * params@dw_full}. That weight is a
normalisation which the kernel construction is built to cancel, not a
quadrature weight, so it is the one place where you must \emph{not} bin-average.
Pairing the point-sampled \code{\link[=pred_kernel]{pred_kernel()}} with a bin-averaged prey weight
applies the prey-bin integral twice.
}

